          "text": "The weak-acid model begins with the equilibrium HA \u21cc H+ + A\u2212 and defines Ka=[H+][A\u2212]/[HA] for idealized activities. Taking negative base-10 logarithms gives pH=pKa+log10([A\u2212]/[HA]). Solving for [HA] divided by total acid yields the plotted protonated fraction. The curve passes exactly through one-half when pH equals pKa; two units above pKa approximately one part in 101 remains protonated. Use the control to compare a solution at pH 3.8, 4.8 and 5.8. Predict the change before reading the calculated label. Then explain why an acidic residue inside a protein need not follow this curve with the pKa of the free amino acid. Electrostatic interactions, solvent exclusion and other titratable groups may change the apparent equilibrium. A titration experiment requires a measurement that distinguishes protonated from deprotonated populations, rather than equating a pH electrode reading with a fraction of one selected molecular species. This mathematical model is valuable precisely because its assumptions are visible: one site, equilibrium, fixed pKa and no explicit activity corrections.\n\nInvestigation challenge: start with pH below pKa, where HA dominates, and then move two pH units above the same pKa. Ask which chemical population becomes more abundant and whether the total number of acid molecules must change. It need not: protonation can redistribute a fixed molecular pool. The plotted number describes an equilibrium fraction, whereas a titration curve may include buffer capacity, dilution and changes in total volume. Design a control where ionic strength is held fixed while pH changes. If the measured curve shifts relative to the model, explain whether apparent pKa, activity corrections or an unrecognized additional ionizable group could account for the discrepancy. Report changes as fractions and percentages carefully; a fraction of 0.01 is one percent, not 0.01 percent."
