          "text": "The urea stoichiometry display treats an input in millimoles of nitrogen atoms that are all disposed of as urea. Because each urea molecule contains two nitrogen atoms, the number of millimoles of urea equals half the input nitrogen amount. This is simple atomic conservation, not a simulation of an intact person. For 6 mmol of nitrogen equivalents, the model outputs 3 mmol urea and, if one also considers energy use, an idealized four high-energy phosphate equivalents per urea corresponds to 12 mmol such equivalents. The real urea cycle draws the two nitrogen atoms from different chemical donors and is localized across cellular compartments. Other nitrogen excretion routes and tissue metabolism make whole-body nitrogen balance more complicated. A teaching experiment should distinguish a chemical amount from the rate of appearance in urine or medium and specify the observation time. Clinical decisions cannot be based on this simplified ledger.\n\nInvestigation challenge: label a sample as containing 6 mmol nitrogen equivalents. If every nitrogen atom appears in urea, the simplified output is 3 mmol urea. Now suppose a mass balance independently detects 1 mmol nitrogen equivalents in ammonium and 1 mmol in amino acids at the end. Only 4 mmol nitrogen equivalents remain assignable to urea, so the maximum under this ledger is 2 mmol urea. This is an example of reducing the model domain when measured alternatives appear. Distinguish atom equivalents from molecular units throughout. Clinically important disorders cannot be inferred from the classroom calculation: the model has no organ physiology, enzyme kinetics or individual health information."
